    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Recent advancements in mRNA therapeutics have focused on refining the lipid nanoparticle (LNP) delivery system to improve endosomal escape and minimize systemic immunogenicity. The integration of ionizable lipids with branched alkyl chains has been shown to increase the pKa-dependent membrane destabilization, facilitating the release of the mRNA cargo into the cytosol post-endocytosis. The ionizable lipids undergo protonation at physiological pH, which enhances their fusogenic properties, leading to the disruption of endosomal membranes. This process is crucial for the successful release of mRNA into the cytoplasm, where it can be translated into the desired protein. Additionally, the inclusion of PEGylated lipids at controlled molar ratios is critical for preventing particle aggregation and modulating circulation time. PEGylation provides a steric barrier that reduces opsonization and subsequent clearance by the mononuclear phagocyte system, thereby enhancing the half-life of the LNPs in circulation. Modulating the stoichiometric ratio of these components significantly impacts the biodistribution and expression levels in hepatic tissues. The precise balance of ionizable and PEGylated lipids influences the LNP's ability to evade the reticuloendothelial system while ensuring",
      "source": "target",
